  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low pressure lifting through the region tonight \nthrough Sunday morning will produce showers and thunderstorms with \nthe potential for flash flooding across portions of the forecast \narea. \n\nHigh pressure has moved offshore with a return flow beginning to \ntake shape across the area. As this continues to occur, low level \nmoisture will increase over the area. Meanwhile, low pressure will \norganize and develop over the Mid-Atlantic. A warm front associated \nwith that low will lift north through the region later this evening \ninto the overnight , and then southerly flow will allow dew points \nto rise well into the 60s and low 70s. PWATs will rise to 2-2.25 \ninches. \n\nLow pressure then follows north through the region late tonight \nthrough Sunday morning. This system will be fairly progressive, \nlifting from Delmarva around midnight tonight to Long Island by \naround midday Sunday. However, due to the high moisture content in \nthe atmosphere, there will be a 6-8 hour window during the early \nmorning hours on Sunday where as much as 1-2 inches of convective \nrain will fall. The primary area of concern is the I-95 corridor and \nsurrounding urban areas and small streams where flash flooding is \nmore possible given any rapid runoff. It is these areas that are \nunder a Flood Watch for flash flooding that begins at midnight \ntonight running until Noon Sunday.  \n\nPrecipitation tapers off Sunday morning as low pressure departs. A \nweak cold front swings through Sunday afternoon behind the low \nfollowed by a secondary cold front Sunday night. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front Thursday into Friday may bring some \nshowers and thunderstorms. \n\nAn upper-level trough across central into eastern Canada is forecast \nto push a cold front into our area at the end of next week. While \nthe guidance, including ensembles, are in decent agreement showing \nthis trough, there are significant differences with the \namplification and therefore speed of the trough. This will have \nimpacts to the cold front itself. If the main energy slides far \nenough north of our area, the front may tend to be slower moving. \nThis scenario would open the door for a greater chance for a round \nof convection. A faster movement to the front would lessen the \nlength of convection over our area. The front may then stall to our \nsouth into next weekend, with one or more possible waves of low \npressure tracking along it with time. A plume of deeper moisture \nlooks to get drawn northward ahead of this front, therefore some \nheavy rain potential may exist with it and this also depends on \nwhether it stalls or not. Confidence remains low during this time \nframe.",
